+Reading from a file
+~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
+
+Use :func:`~rich.progress.open` to read a file while a progress bar shows how many bytes have been read. This is useful for long JSON or CSV parses. By default the file is opened in text mode, and the ``total`` is the size of the file in bytes::
+
+    from rich.progress import open
+
+    with open("data.json") as f:
+        text = f.read()
+
+Pass ``"rb"`` as the mode to read bytes instead of text::
+
+    from rich.progress import open
+
+    with open("data.bin", "rb") as f:
+        data = f.read()
+
+The progress display starts when the ``with`` block is entered and stops when it exits, and the file is closed on exit. The ``total`` argument can be given explicitly as a byte count if you know it differs from the file size.
+
+Reading from a file-like object
+~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
+
+If you already have an open binary file-like object, use :func:`~rich.progress.wrap_file`. It only supports binary reads, and you must give the ``total`` number of bytes. The reader does not close the file you pass in::
+
+    import io
+    from rich.progress import wrap_file
+
+    payload = b"example data\n" * 1000
+    source = io.BytesIO(payload)
+
+    with wrap_file(source, len(payload)) as f:
+        data = f.read()
+
+    print(source.closed)  # False
+
+When you use :meth:`~rich.progress.Progress.wrap_file` on an existing :class:`~rich.progress.Progress` instance, you can omit ``total`` if the task you pass with ``task_id`` already has a total. Without a total, a ``ValueError`` is raised.
+
